#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105513 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f88dd688-449b-5a6d-8b91-78d00d0c932b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of the Triangle Spanned by Two Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "957d070a-edc8-521f-a5bd-d10177a0bdbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-4344" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 4,0,-3 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 5,-1,2 \\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\mathbf{a} \\times  \\mathbf{b} & = \\left\\vert \\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 4 & 0 & - 3 \\\\ 5 & - 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right\\vert \\\\  & = \\left\\vert \\begin{array}{cc}0 & - 3 \\\\ - 1 & 2\\end{array}\\right\\vert \\mathbf{i} - \\left\\vert \\begin{array}{cc}4 & - 3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{array}\\right\\vert \\mathbf{j} + \\left\\vert \\begin{array}{cc}4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & - 1\\end{array}\\right\\vert \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = ( 0 \\cdot  2 - ( - 3 ) \\cdot  ( - 1 ) ) \\mathbf{i} - ( 4 \\cdot  2 - ( - 3 ) \\cdot  5 ) \\mathbf{j} + ( 4 \\cdot  ( - 1 ) - 0 \\cdot  5 ) \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = - 3 \\mathbf{i} - 23 \\mathbf{j} - 4 \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = \\langle  - 3 , - 23 , - 4 \\rangle  .\\end{array}$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}A & = | \\, \\mathbf{a} \\times  \\mathbf{b} \\, | \\\\  & = \\sqrt{( - 3 {)}^{2} + ( - 23 {)}^{2} + ( - 4 {)}^{2}} \\\\  & = \\sqrt{554} .\\end{array}$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{554}}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105514 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c30217a-484c-5c31-a2bf-413f04e0edea" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area of a Triangle Given Its Vertices"}] :question/id #uuid "e354c43b-837f-5447-9759-6aeb3952cd57" :question/math-academy-id "e-4345" :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$, if $A(1,0,-2)$, $B(-2,0,1)$, $C(1,1,-1)$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\overrightarrow{AB} & = \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a} \\\\  & = ( - 2 \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{k} ) - ( \\mathbf{i} - 2 \\mathbf{k} ) \\\\  & = - 3 \\mathbf{i} + 3 \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = \\langle  - 3 , 0 , 3 \\rangle \\\\ \\overrightarrow{AC} & = \\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{a} \\\\  & = ( \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} - \\mathbf{k} ) - ( \\mathbf{i} - 2 \\mathbf{k} ) \\\\  & = \\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = \\langle  0 , 1 , 1 \\rangle\\end{array}$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\overrightarrow{AB} \\times  \\overrightarrow{AC} & = \\left\\vert \\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ - 3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right\\vert \\\\  & = \\left\\vert \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right\\vert \\mathbf{i} - \\left\\vert \\begin{array}{cc}- 3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right\\vert \\mathbf{j} + \\left\\vert \\begin{array}{cc}- 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right\\vert \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = ( 0 \\cdot  1 - 3 \\cdot  1 ) \\mathbf{i} - ( - 3 \\cdot  1 - 3 \\cdot  0 ) \\mathbf{j} + ( - 3 \\cdot  1 - 0 \\cdot  0 ) \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = - 3 \\mathbf{i} + 3 \\mathbf{j} - 3 \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = \\langle  - 3 , 3 , - 3 \\rangle  .\\end{array}$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}A & = | \\overrightarrow{AB} \\times  \\overrightarrow{AC} | \\\\  & = \\sqrt{( - 3 {)}^{2} + {3}^{2} + ( - 3 {)}^{2}} \\\\  & = 3 \\sqrt{3} .\\end{array}$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105515 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9331987e-8676-5bdb-b4ce-2129b1b09a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Altitude of a Triangle Given Its Vertices"}] :question/id #uuid "23793048-621c-5ab3-97ac-7e635553c947" :question/math-academy-id "e-4346" :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ if $A(-1,1,0)$, $B(1,1,2)$, $C(1,-1,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2} CH \\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH$, we first find $A_{\\bigtriangleup}$. To start, we find the vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\overrightarrow{AB} & = \\mathbf{b} - \\mathbf{a} \\\\  & = ( \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} + 2 \\mathbf{k} ) - ( - \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} ) \\\\  & = 2 \\mathbf{i} + 2 \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = \\langle  2 , 0 , 2 \\rangle \\\\ \\overrightarrow{AC} & = \\mathbf{c} - \\mathbf{a} \\\\  & = ( \\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k} ) - ( - \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} ) \\\\  & = 2 \\mathbf{i} - 2 \\mathbf{j} + \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = \\langle  2 , - 2 , 1 \\rangle\\end{array}$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\overrightarrow{AB} \\times  \\overrightarrow{AC} & = \\left\\vert \\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & - 2 & 1\\end{array}\\right\\vert \\\\  & = \\left\\vert \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 2 \\\\ - 2 & 1\\end{array}\\right\\vert \\mathbf{i} - \\left\\vert \\begin{array}{cc}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{array}\\right\\vert \\mathbf{j} + \\left\\vert \\begin{array}{cc}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & - 2\\end{array}\\right\\vert \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = ( 0 \\cdot  1 - 2 \\cdot  ( - 2 ) ) \\mathbf{i} - ( 2 \\cdot  1 - 2 \\cdot  2 ) \\mathbf{j} + ( 2 \\cdot  ( - 2 ) - 0 \\cdot  2 ) \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = 4 \\mathbf{i} + 2 \\mathbf{j} - 4 \\mathbf{k} \\\\  & = \\langle  4 , 2 , - 4 \\rangle  .\\end{array}$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}A & = | \\overrightarrow{AB} \\times  \\overrightarrow{AC} | \\\\  & = \\sqrt{{4}^{2} + {2}^{2} + ( - 4 {)}^{2}} \\\\  & = \\sqrt{36} \\\\  & = 6.\\end{array}$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 = 3\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}A B & = | \\overrightarrow{AB} | \\\\  & = \\sqrt{{2}^{2} + {0}^{2} + {2}^{2}} \\\\  & = \\sqrt{8} \\\\  & = 2 \\sqrt{2}\\end{array}$$\n\nFinally, using the formula\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2} CH \\cdot AB\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{△} &= \\frac{1}{2}CH \\cdot AB \\\\\n3 &= \\frac{1}{2}CH \\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\\n3 &= CH \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\\nCH &= \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\\nCH &= \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9331987e-8676-5bdb-b4ce-2129b1b09a8c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Altitude of a Triangle Given Its Vertices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac9d52fe-7c33-50cd-8d6c-8592f12d256b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102532} {:answer/id #uuid "d80a8528-7abd-5eda-b23c-63c9f10d1c3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102533} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0e7209-7d0e-56e7-8460-67c34fd37dae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102534} {:answer/id #uuid "0612b416-9a0b-5f62-b5d3-c94256bc45e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102535} {:answer/id #uuid "17c50b66-6232-5759-8604-c579767507ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d80a8528-7abd-5eda-b23c-63c9f10d1c3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102533} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c58cdd9-a8f0-5eea-8cd1-717ac742f5d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102531}] :question/id #uuid "95f6f3a1-caa9-5902-b6fb-cb14398f3fb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-20027" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nCalculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△ABC$, if $A(2, 1, 2)$, $B(1, 2, 1)$, $C(3, 2, 3)$." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
